  "content": {
    "id": "p44-q49-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "49",
    "prompt": "Two of the roots of the polynomial equation $px^3+qx^2+rx+s=0$ sum to zero.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p44-q49-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the remaining root is $-\\dfrac{q}{p}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The remaining root is $-\\dfrac qp$, since the other two roots sum to zero.",
          "worked": "Let the roots be $\\alpha$, $-\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. Using the sum of roots,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha-\\alpha+\\beta&=-\\frac qp\\\\\\beta&=-\\frac qp.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q49-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $ps=qr$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$ps=qr$.\n\nThe root relationships give $r=-p\\alpha^2$ and $s=-q\\alpha^2$.",
          "worked": "Using the sum of roots in pairs,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac rp&=\\alpha(-\\alpha)+\\alpha\\beta-\\alpha\\beta\\\\\\frac rp&=-\\alpha^2\\\\r&=-p\\alpha^2.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the product of roots and $\\beta=-\\frac{q}{p}$,\n$$\\begin{align*}-\\alpha^2\\beta&=-\\frac sp\\\\s&=p\\alpha^2\\beta\\\\s&=-q\\alpha^2.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore\n$$\\begin{align*}ps&=-pq\\alpha^2\\\\qr&=-pq\\alpha^2.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $ps=qr$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 102
      }
    ]
  },
